    "summary": "Legendary WR Prelude → Dark Star marathon with soaring Garcia",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a monster Weather Report Suite Prelude → Dark Star → Eyes of the World → Playing in the Band sequence and tight, melodic playing throughout. Jerry’s guitar is on fire from the first‑set opener through the epic second set, while Phil lays down solid bass foundations. The Miller soundboard capture delivers pristine audio for the full 30‑song marathon.",
    "review": "On March 28, 1973 at the Springfield Civic Center the Dead delivered one of their most memorable spring outings. A rousing first set opened with “Cumberland Blues” and “Here Comes Sunshine,” then rolled through classics like “Jack Straw,” “Box of Rain,” “Looks Like Rain,” and a seamless “China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider.” The second set was the show’s centerpiece: a Weather Report Suite Prelude that exploded into an expansive Dark Star, flowed straight into “Eyes Of The World” and culminated in a soaring “Playing In The Band” before closing with a rollicking “Johnny B. Goode.” Across every recording Jerry Garcia’s tone is described as inventive, hot, and almost cosmic—especially on the marathon Dark Star and his expressive solo on “Looks Like Rain.” Phil Lesh provides melodic, steady bass work that shines in the deep‑space jams, while Bob Weir’s rhythmic leads lock tightly with Jerry, notably on “Me And My Uncle” and “Jack Straw.” Keith Godchaux’s piano and organ add clear, volume‑pedal texture that underpins the 1973 sound. Bill Kreutzmann’s one‑drummer groove drives the band forward, though a few audience tapes wish his mix were louder. The Miller SBD (gd1973-03-28.sbd.miller.86459.sbeok.flac16) stands out for its pristine, balanced mix with only a minute‑long glitch, making it the definitive listening source.",
